    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm having some trouble with a few recent dermatology claims. We're seeing a lot of atypical presentations that don't seem to fit the standard L-series codes perfectly. Specifically, when a patient presents with irregular, nodular growths that have a somewhat greenish or mottled discoloration, but it doesn't clearly meet the criteria for a specific malignancy or a common benign lesion, what's the best way to approach the hierarchy? Should we be looking at more specific morphology codes or just defaulting to \"other specified\" under the L-category?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For atypical, irregularly pigmented, nodular growths in dermatology, you should first thoroughly review the clinical documentation for any suggestive features that might point toward a less common condition. Before defaulting to \"other specified,\" examine the morphology codes to see if any descriptions of irregular, mottled pigmentation fit better, even if they aren't classic fits for common diagnoses. If the clinical picture remains equivocal and does not align with established code criteria, then using \"other specified\" under the appropriate L-series category is the most defensible coding approach.",
      "source": "target",
