    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but I'm specifically worried about the audit trail. We've had a few cases where the morphology looks almost... unnatural? Like, the texture is very bumpy and the skin seems almost thickened or leathery in a way that doesn't follow typical inflammatory patterns. If the physician's notes still don't give a clear diagnosis but they describe these \"strange, non-human looking\" characteristics, how do I justify the code selection to the payers without it being flagged for inaccuracy?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When descriptions are notably subjective, such as \"non-human looking,\" your primary defense for the audit trail must remain on the objective elements documented by the physician. Focus on translating those descriptive terms into established medical terminology like \"indurated,\" \"firm/leathery consistency,\" or \"hyperkeratotic\" within the clinical notes. If the documentation only provides vague, subjective descriptors without supporting diagnostic evidence, accurately coding the uncertainty using an appropriate \"unspecified\" or \"other specified\" code, while documenting your rationale for the ambiguity, is the safest path to avoid an inaccuracy flag.",
      "source": "target",
